/* Daily Text Developer: 40 original informational texts, each at three English proficiency levels.
   Row: [subject, title, language function, Levels 1-2 text, Levels 3-4 text, Level 5 text, [[word, meaning] x4], [question x3]] */
const TXRAW=[
["SCI","The Water Cycle","sequence",
"Water moves all the time. The sun heats water in rivers and oceans. The water evaporates. It becomes vapor, a gas in the air. High in the sky, the vapor cools. It condenses into clouds. Then precipitation falls as rain or snow. The water cycle starts again.",
"Earth's water is always moving in a cycle. First, the sun heats water in oceans, lakes, and rivers, and some of it evaporates. The water becomes vapor, a gas that rises into the air. Next, the vapor cools high in the sky and condenses into tiny drops that form clouds. When the drops grow heavy, precipitation falls as rain, snow, or hail. Finally, the water flows back to rivers and oceans, and the cycle begins again.",
"The water on Earth today is the same water that has been here for millions of years, because it moves through a cycle instead of being used up. Energy from the sun causes liquid water in oceans, lakes, and soil to evaporate into water vapor, an invisible gas. As the vapor rises, the air around it grows colder, so the vapor condenses into the tiny droplets that make up clouds. When the droplets join together and become too heavy to stay in the air, they fall as precipitation: rain, snow, sleet, or hail. Some of this water soaks into the ground, and the rest runs downhill into streams and rivers that carry it back to the ocean.",
[["evaporate","to change from a liquid into a gas"],["vapor","water in the form of a gas"],["condense","to change from a gas into a liquid"],["precipitation","water that falls from clouds, such as rain or snow"]],
["What heats the water?","What happens to the vapor when it cools?","Where have you seen part of the water cycle? Tell a partner."]],
["SCI","How Plants Make Food","explain",
"Plants make their own food. They need three things. They need sunlight. They need water. They need carbon dioxide, a gas in the air. The leaf uses them to make sugar. Sugar is food for the plant. The plant also gives off oxygen. People and animals breathe oxygen.",
"Plants do not eat food the way animals do. They make their own food in a process called photosynthesis. Roots take in water from the soil, and leaves take in carbon dioxide, a gas, from the air. Leaves also capture sunlight. The plant uses the energy in sunlight to turn water and carbon dioxide into sugar, which it uses to live and grow. The process also releases oxygen, the gas that people and animals need to breathe.",
"Almost every food chain on Earth begins with photosynthesis, the process plants use to make their own food. A green substance in leaves, called chlorophyll, captures energy from sunlight. The plant uses that energy to combine water, which travels up from the roots, with carbon dioxide, which enters through tiny openings in the leaves. The result is sugar, a form of stored energy the plant can use to grow or save for later. Oxygen is released as a by-product. This means plants do two jobs that animals depend on: they produce food at the base of the food chain, and they replace the oxygen that animals remove from the air.",
[["sunlight","light from the sun"],["carbon dioxide","a gas in the air that plants use"],["oxygen","a gas in the air that people and animals breathe"],["sugar","the food a plant makes and uses for energy"]],
["What three things does a plant need to make food?","Why is oxygen important?","Explain to a partner how a plant makes food."]],
["SCI","Why the Moon Changes Shape","explain",
"The Moon does not make light. It reflects light from the Sun. The Moon moves around Earth. This path is its orbit. One orbit takes about one month. Each night we see a different part of the bright side. Each shape is a phase. A full moon looks like a circle.",
"The Moon seems to change shape, but it is always a ball. The Moon does not make its own light. It reflects light from the Sun, so one half is always bright. As the Moon travels in its orbit around Earth, we see different amounts of that bright half. Each shape we see is called a phase. When we see the whole bright side, it is a full moon. The pattern repeats about every 29 days.",
"People have watched the Moon change shape for thousands of years, and many calendars are based on it. The explanation is simple: the Moon does not produce light. It can only reflect sunlight, so the half that faces the Sun is always lit. Because the Moon moves in an orbit around Earth, our view of the lit half changes from night to night. When the Moon is between Earth and the Sun, the lit side faces away from us and we see almost nothing, a phase called the new moon. About two weeks later, the Moon is on the opposite side of Earth, and we see a full moon. The whole pattern takes about 29 and a half days.",
[["orbit","the path one object takes around another"],["reflect","to send light back"],["phase","one of the shapes of the Moon that we see"],["full moon","the phase when we see the whole bright side"]],
["Does the Moon make its own light?","Why do we see different shapes?","Describe the last Moon you saw. Which phase was it?"]],
["SCI","Why the Ground Shakes","cause",
"Earth's crust is its hard top layer. The crust is broken into big pieces. Each piece is a plate. Plates move very slowly. Sometimes two plates get stuck. A crack between them is a fault. Then the plates slip fast. Energy moves through the ground. The ground shakes. This is an earthquake.",
"The ground feels still, but Earth's crust, its hard outer layer, is broken into huge pieces called plates. The plates move a few centimeters each year. Where two plates meet, there are cracks called faults. The rough edges of the plates can get stuck while the rest keeps moving, so pressure builds. When the rock finally slips, the stored energy is released and travels through the ground in waves. Because of these waves, the ground shakes in an earthquake.",
"An earthquake can last less than a minute, yet it is the result of pressure that has built up for many years. Earth's crust is divided into large plates that move slowly, about as fast as fingernails grow. Along a fault, the boundary where two blocks of rock meet, friction holds the edges in place while the plates continue to move. As a result, the rock bends and stores energy like a stretched rubber band. When the stress becomes greater than the friction, the rock breaks loose and the energy spreads outward as waves. Scientists cannot predict the exact day of an earthquake, so engineers design buildings that bend without collapsing.",
[["crust","the hard outer layer of Earth"],["plate","a huge piece of Earth's outer layer"],["fault","a crack in rock where two blocks can move"],["energy","the power to move or change things"]],
["What is a plate?","What causes the ground to shake?","Why do engineers study earthquakes? Use because."]],
["SCI","Who Eats What","sequence",
"All living things need energy. A plant is a producer. It makes food from sunlight. A consumer eats other living things. A rabbit eats the plant. A fox eats the rabbit. Energy moves from one to the next. This is a food chain. A decomposer breaks down dead things. It makes the soil rich.",
"A food chain shows how energy moves from one living thing to another. It starts with a producer, such as grass, which makes its own food from sunlight. Next comes a consumer that eats plants, such as a rabbit. Then a second consumer, such as a fox, eats the rabbit. When plants and animals die, a decomposer, such as a mushroom, breaks them down and returns nutrients to the soil. New plants use those nutrients to grow.",
"Every ecosystem runs on energy that begins with the sun. A producer, such as a plant, captures that energy and stores it as food. A consumer cannot do this, so it must eat other organisms: a grasshopper eats grass, a frog eats the grasshopper, and a snake eats the frog. At each step, most of the energy is used for living or lost as heat, which is why an ecosystem has many plants but only a few top predators. A decomposer, such as a fungus, completes the chain by breaking down dead organisms and returning nutrients to the soil. If one link disappears, every other link is affected.",
[["energy","the power living things need to live and grow"],["producer","a living thing that makes its own food"],["consumer","a living thing that eats other living things"],["decomposer","a living thing that breaks down dead plants and animals"]],
["What does a producer make?","What does a decomposer do?","Tell a food chain with three living things. Use first, next, and then."]],
["SCI","Solid, Liquid, Gas","compare",
"Matter is made of tiny particles. Matter has three common states. A solid keeps its shape. Ice is a solid. A liquid flows. It takes the shape of its cup. Water is a liquid. A gas spreads out to fill a space. Steam is a gas. Heat can change one state to another.",
"Everything around you is matter, and matter is made of tiny particles. In a solid, such as ice, the particles are packed closely and only vibrate, so a solid keeps its shape. In a liquid, such as water, the particles slide past each other, so a liquid flows and takes the shape of its container. In a gas, such as steam, the particles are far apart and move quickly, so a gas spreads out to fill any space. Adding or removing heat changes one state to another.",
"Ice, water, and steam look very different, but they are the same substance in three states. The difference is how the particles move. In a solid, particles are held tightly together and can only vibrate in place, which gives the solid a fixed shape and volume. In a liquid, particles stay close but slide past one another, so a liquid keeps its volume but takes the shape of its container. In a gas, particles have enough energy to move freely and far apart, so a gas has neither a fixed shape nor a fixed volume. Heating a substance gives its particles more energy, while cooling removes energy, and that is why matter melts, boils, condenses, and freezes.",
[["particles","the tiny pieces that make up all matter"],["solid","matter that keeps its own shape"],["liquid","matter that flows and takes the shape of its container"],["gas","matter that spreads out to fill a space"]],
["Name the three states of matter.","How is a liquid different from a solid?","Compare ice and steam. Use both and but."]],
["SCI","Your Heart at Work","explain",
"Your heart is a muscle. It is about the size of your fist. The heart is a pump. It pushes blood around your body. Blood carries oxygen to every part. You can feel your heart work. Touch your wrist. The small beat is your pulse. Exercise makes your heart strong.",
"Your heart is a muscle about the size of your fist, and it works like a pump. Each beat pushes blood through tubes called blood vessels. First, the heart sends blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen. Then the blood returns to the heart, which pumps it out to the rest of the body. Every beat makes a small wave you can feel at your wrist or neck, called a pulse. When you exercise, your muscles need more oxygen, so your heart beats faster.",
"The heart is a muscle that never rests. It beats about 100,000 times a day to pump blood through the body. The right side of the heart sends blood to the lungs, where the blood picks up oxygen and gets rid of carbon dioxide. The left side then pumps this oxygen-rich blood through arteries to every organ and muscle. Veins carry the used blood back, and the trip begins again. You can measure the work of the heart by counting your pulse, the wave of pressure that each beat sends through the arteries. During exercise the pulse rises because working muscles demand more oxygen, and regular exercise makes the heart muscle stronger.",
[["pump","something that pushes a liquid from place to place"],["blood","the red liquid that moves through your body"],["oxygen","a gas from the air that your body needs"],["pulse","the beat you feel each time your heart pushes blood"]],
["How big is your heart?","Why does your heart beat faster when you run?","Count your pulse for 15 seconds. Explain what you are feeling."]],
["SCI","How a Hurricane Grows","cause",
"A hurricane is a very big storm. It starts over warm ocean water. Warm, wet air rises. More air moves in. The wind begins to spin. The storm grows. Strong wind can break trees and roofs. The wind also pushes ocean water onto land. This flood is a storm surge. People near the coast must be ready.",
"A hurricane is a huge spinning storm that forms over warm ocean water. The warm water heats the air above it, so the air rises and carries water vapor with it. More air rushes in to take its place, and the wind begins to spin. As long as the storm stays over warm water, it keeps growing. When a hurricane reaches land, its wind can damage buildings. Its most dangerous part is often the storm surge, a rise of ocean water that floods the coast.",
"Hurricanes are among the most powerful storms on Earth, and the Gulf Coast, including Mississippi, has a long history with them. A hurricane needs warm ocean water as fuel. The water evaporates, the warm, moist air rises, and as it cools it releases heat that makes the air rise even faster. Because Earth rotates, the wind moving toward the center begins to spin around a calm area called the eye. Scientists rank each storm from Category 1 to Category 5 by wind speed. However, wind is not the only danger. A storm surge, the wall of ocean water that the hurricane pushes onto land, and heavy rain cause most of the flooding, which is why officials sometimes order people to leave the coast.",
[["storm","very bad weather with strong wind and rain"],["warm","a little hot"],["wind","air that moves"],["surge","a fast rise of ocean water onto land"]],
["Where does a hurricane start?","What is a storm surge?","How can a family get ready for a big storm? Explain why."]],
["SCI","Energy That Runs Out and Energy That Does Not","compare",
"We use energy every day. Some energy comes from fuel like coal, oil, and gas. We burn the fuel. These sources are nonrenewable. One day they will run out. Other energy is renewable. It does not run out. Solar energy comes from the sun. Wind energy comes from moving air. Both kinds of energy make electricity.",
"The electricity in your school comes from an energy source. Some sources are nonrenewable. Coal, oil, and natural gas are fuels that formed underground over millions of years, and once we burn them they are gone. Other sources are renewable, which means nature replaces them quickly. Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity, and wind turbines use moving air. Both kinds of sources can power a city, but they are different in cost, in pollution, and in how steady they are.",
"Every energy source has benefits and costs. Nonrenewable sources, such as coal, oil, and natural gas, are fuels that took millions of years to form. They can produce electricity at any hour, but the supply is limited and burning them releases carbon dioxide and other pollution. Renewable sources, such as solar, wind, and moving water, are replaced by nature and release little pollution while they run. On the other hand, solar panels produce nothing at night and wind turbines stop in calm weather, so these sources need batteries or backup power. For this reason, most places use a mix of sources, and communities must decide which mix is best for them.",
[["fuel","something we burn to get energy"],["nonrenewable","not replaced after we use it"],["renewable","replaced by nature, so it does not run out"],["solar","from the sun"]],
["Name two renewable energy sources.","Why will coal and oil run out?","Which energy source is best for our town? Give one reason."]],
["SCI","Soap Against Germs","argue",
"Germs are very small living things. You cannot see them. Some germs make people sick. Germs spread when we touch things. They get on our hands. Then we touch our eyes, nose, or mouth. Soap and water wash germs away. Wash for 20 seconds. Clean hands help you stay healthy.",
"Germs are tiny living things that are too small to see. Most are harmless, but some cause illnesses such as colds and the flu. Germs spread when people cough, sneeze, or touch the same objects. Then the germs move from our hands to our eyes, nose, or mouth. Washing with soap and water is one of the best ways to stop them. Soap loosens the germs, and running water carries them away. Health experts recommend scrubbing for at least 20 seconds to stay healthy.",
"One of the most effective tools in medicine costs almost nothing: washing your hands. Germs, including bacteria and viruses, spread easily from surfaces to hands and from hands to the eyes, nose, and mouth. Water alone does not remove them well because many germs cling to the natural oils on skin. Soap solves this problem. Its molecules attach to both oil and water, so scrubbing lifts the germs off the skin and rinsing carries them down the drain. Health experts recommend washing for at least 20 seconds, especially before eating and after using the restroom. When more people wash their hands, fewer germs spread through a school, and more students stay healthy and in class.",
[["germs","tiny living things that can make people sick"],["spread","to move from one person or place to another"],["soap","what we use with water to clean our hands"],["healthy","well, not sick"]],
["How do germs spread?","Why is soap better than water alone?","Convince a classmate to wash before lunch. Give two reasons."]],

["SS","The Mississippi River","describe",
"The Mississippi River is very long. It starts in Minnesota. It flows south to the Gulf. It touches ten states. Mississippi is one of them. Boats carry food and goods on the river. This helps trade. Near the Gulf, the river drops soil. This new land is a delta.",
"The Mississippi River is one of the longest rivers in North America. It begins at a small lake in Minnesota and flows south for more than 2,300 miles to the Gulf. Along the way it touches ten states and forms the western border of Mississippi. For centuries people have used the river for trade, and today barges still carry grain, fuel, and other goods. Near its mouth the river slows down and drops the soil it carries, building a wide, flat area called a delta.",
"The Mississippi River has shaped the land, the economy, and the culture of the central United States. It begins at Lake Itasca in Minnesota and flows south for more than 2,300 miles, collecting water from rivers such as the Missouri and the Ohio. Native peoples traveled and traded on it long before Europeans arrived, and in the 1800s steamboats turned river towns such as Natchez and Vicksburg into busy ports. Trade still depends on the river: barges move huge loads of grain and fuel at low cost. The river also floods. Over thousands of years those floods spread rich soil across the land beside it and built the delta at its mouth, which is why the region became a center of farming.",
[["river","a long body of moving water"],["flows","moves along, like water"],["trade","buying and selling goods"],["delta","flat land a river builds where it meets the sea"]],
["Where does the river start?","Why is the river important for trade?","Describe a river, lake, or ocean you know. What do people use it for?"]],
["SS","Three Branches of Government","explain",
"The United States government has three parts. Each part is a branch. Congress is the first branch. Congress makes each law. The President leads the second branch. This branch carries out the law. A court is in the third branch. The courts decide what the law means. No branch has all the power.",
"The Constitution divides the United States government into three branches. The legislative branch is Congress, which writes each law. The executive branch is led by the President, who carries out the laws. The judicial branch is the court system, which decides what a law means and whether it follows the Constitution. Each branch can limit the other two. For example, the President can reject a law, and a court can strike one down. This system keeps any one branch from becoming too powerful.",
"The people who wrote the Constitution in 1787 had lived under a king, and they did not want one person or group to hold all the power. Their solution was to divide the government into three branches. Congress, the legislative branch, writes each law and controls spending. The President leads the executive branch, which enforces the laws and directs the military. The judicial branch, headed by the Supreme Court, interprets the laws. In addition, each branch can check the others. The President may veto a law, Congress may override the veto with a two-thirds vote, and a court may rule that a law violates the Constitution. This arrangement, called checks and balances, forces the branches to share power.",
[["branch","one of the three parts of the government"],["law","a rule that everyone must follow"],["Congress","the group of people who make laws for the country"],["court","the place where judges decide what the law means"]],
["Which branch makes laws?","Why did the writers divide the government?","Explain one way a branch can stop another branch."]],
["SS","The Bill of Rights","summarize",
"The Constitution is the plan for the United States government. An amendment is a change to it. The first ten are the Bill of Rights. They protect the rights of the people. One right is freedom of speech. Another is freedom of religion. People also have the right to a fair trial. These rights belong to everyone here.",
"The Bill of Rights is the name for the first ten amendments, or changes, to the Constitution. It was added in 1791 to protect the rights of the people from the power of the government. The First Amendment protects freedom of speech, religion, and the press. Other amendments protect people accused of crimes. For example, a person has the right to a lawyer and to a fair trial by a jury. These rights are still used in courts every day.",
"When the Constitution was written, many Americans worried that the new national government would be too strong. Several states agreed to approve it only if a list of rights was added. The result was the Bill of Rights, the first ten amendments, approved in 1791. The First Amendment protects freedom of religion, speech, the press, and peaceful protest. Others protect people from unreasonable searches and guarantee a fair and speedy trial. The amendments do not give rights to the people. Instead, they stop the government from taking away rights the people already have. Courts still argue about how to apply these words, written more than two hundred years ago, to modern life.",
[["amendment","a change added to the Constitution"],["rights","things every person is allowed to do or have"],["freedom","the power to act, speak, or think as you choose"],["protect","to keep safe"]],
["How many amendments are in the Bill of Rights?","Why did people want a list of rights?","Which right is most important to you? Tell why."]],
["SS","Why Prices Change","cause",
"A price is what you pay. Why do prices change? Supply is how much of a thing there is. Demand is how much people want it. Many buyers want new shoes. There are only a few. Demand is high. Supply is low. So the price goes up. When there are too many shoes, the price goes down.",
"Prices change because of supply and demand. Supply is the amount of a product that sellers have. Demand is the amount that buyers want. When many buyers want something and there is not much of it, sellers can raise the price. When stores have more than buyers want, sellers lower the price to sell it. For example, strawberries cost less in spring, when farms pick many of them, and more in winter, when the supply is small.",
"No one person sets the price of gasoline or a concert ticket. In a market, price is the result of supply and demand. Supply is the amount producers are willing to sell, and demand is the amount buyers are willing to purchase. If demand rises while supply stays the same, buyers compete and the price climbs. As a result, producers make more, and the higher price causes some buyers to choose something else. If supply is greater than demand, sellers cut the price to attract buyers. Over time the price moves toward a point where the amount for sale matches the amount people want. Weather, new technology, and trends can all shift supply or demand and change the price again.",
[["price","the money you pay for something"],["supply","how much of a product there is to sell"],["demand","how much of a product people want to buy"],["buyers","people who pay for something"]],
["What is demand?","Why do strawberries cost less in spring?","Tell about a time a price went up or down. What caused it?"]],
["SS","Finding Any Place on Earth","explain",
"A map shows where places are. Many maps have lines. Lines of latitude go east and west. They look flat. The equator is the line around the middle of Earth. Lines of longitude go from the top to the bottom. They meet at the North Pole and the South Pole. Two numbers tell where a place is.",
"A map or globe uses a grid of imaginary lines to show exact locations. Lines of latitude run east and west and measure how far north or south a place is from the equator, the line around the middle of Earth. Lines of longitude run from the North Pole to the South Pole and measure how far east or west a place is. Each line has a number in degrees. Together, one latitude and one longitude give the address of any place on Earth.",
"Before satellites, sailors needed a way to describe a position on an ocean with no landmarks. The answer was a grid of imaginary lines. Latitude lines circle the globe parallel to the equator, which is 0 degrees, and they measure distance north or south up to 90 degrees at each pole. Longitude lines run from pole to pole and measure distance east or west of the prime meridian, a line that passes through Greenwich, England. Any location can be written as a pair of numbers called coordinates. Jackson, Mississippi, for example, is near 32 degrees north latitude and 90 degrees west longitude. The map on a phone uses the same system.",
[["map","a drawing that shows where places are"],["equator","the imaginary line around the middle of Earth"],["latitude","lines that measure how far north or south a place is"],["longitude","lines that measure how far east or west a place is"]],
["What is the equator?","Which lines measure north and south?","Explain how two numbers can show one place."]],
["SS","How a Bill Becomes a Law","sequence",
"A bill is an idea for a new law. First, a member of Congress writes the bill. Next, a small group studies it. Then the House and the Senate vote. Both must say yes. Last, the bill goes to the President. The President can sign it. Then the bill is a law.",
"A law begins as a bill, a written idea for a new law. First, a member of Congress introduces the bill. Next, a committee studies it and may change it. Then the members of that chamber debate and vote. If the bill passes, it goes to the other chamber, and the House and the Senate must agree on the same words. Finally, the bill goes to the President, who can sign it into law or veto it. Congress can still pass a vetoed bill with a two-thirds vote.",
"Thousands of bills are introduced in Congress every year, but only a small share become laws, because the process is designed to be slow. First, a member of the House or the Senate introduces a bill. It is then sent to a committee, where members hold hearings, make changes, and decide whether to send it forward. Most bills end there. If a bill survives, the full chamber debates it and takes a vote. After that, the other chamber repeats the process, and both must approve identical language. Finally, the President may sign the bill or veto it. A veto can be overridden only if two-thirds of each chamber vote to do so. Each step gives people a chance to improve the bill or stop it.",
[["bill","a written idea for a new law"],["Congress","the House and the Senate, which make laws"],["vote","to choose yes or no"],["sign","to write your name to approve something"]],
["What is a bill?","What can the President do with a bill?","Retell the steps. Use first, next, then, and finally."]],
["SS","The Great Migration","cause",
"A migration is a big move of people. Long ago, many Black families lived in the South. Life there was not fair. Laws kept people apart. Pay was low. Factories in the North had jobs. So millions of people moved north. Many left Mississippi for Chicago. They took their music and food with them.",
"The Great Migration was the movement of about six million Black Americans out of the South between about 1910 and 1970. They left for several reasons. Segregation laws treated them unfairly, violence was a constant danger, and farm work paid very little. At the same time, factories in the North and West needed workers and offered better jobs. Many families from Mississippi took the train north to Chicago. As a result, northern cities grew, and music such as the blues spread across the country.",
"Between about 1910 and 1970, roughly six million Black Americans left the South in one of the largest movements of people in United States history. Historians call it the Great Migration. Families were pushed out by segregation laws, the threat of violence, and a farming system that kept them in debt. They were pulled toward the North and West by factory jobs, especially during the two world wars, and by the hope of voting and sending their children to better schools. Railroad lines shaped the routes, so many Mississippians settled in Chicago. The migration changed the nation. It carried the blues to northern cities, where it grew into new styles of music, and it gave Black voters new political power.",
[["migration","the movement of many people from one place to another"],["South","the southern part of the United States"],["North","the northern part of the United States"],["jobs","work that people are paid to do"]],
["Where did many people from Mississippi move?","Give two reasons families left the South.","Why do families move today? Compare with the reasons in the text."]],
["SS","How Voting Works","explain",
"In an election, people choose a leader. Each person can vote one time. A citizen who is 18 or older can vote. First, you sign up. This is called registering. On election day, you go to a voting place. You mark your choices. Your vote is secret. In most elections, the person with the most votes wins.",
"In a democracy, people choose their leaders in an election. In the United States, a citizen who is at least 18 years old can vote. First, a person must register, which means signing up with the state. On election day, voters go to a polling place or, in some states, mail in a ballot. Each vote is secret, and each voter gets one. People vote for leaders such as mayors, governors, and the President. They also vote on some local questions, such as money for schools.",
"Voting is the main way people in a democracy tell the government what they want. In the United States today, a citizen who is 18 or older may vote, but that was not always true. At first, most states allowed only white men who owned property to vote. Amendments to the Constitution later opened the election process to more people: Black men in 1870, women in 1920, and 18-year-olds in 1971. Even then, some states used unfair tests to keep Black citizens from voting until the Voting Rights Act of 1965 banned them. Each leader chosen in an election, from a school board member to the President, makes decisions that affect daily life.",
[["election","the time when people choose their leaders"],["vote","to make your choice in an election"],["citizen","a person who belongs to a country by law"],["leader","a person who guides a group or a government"]],
["How old must a voter be?","What does it mean to register?","Why is a secret vote important? Explain."]],
["SS","Rivers and the First Cities","compare",
"A civilization is a large group of people with cities and laws. The first ones began near a river. Egypt grew by the Nile River. Mesopotamia grew by two rivers. Each year a flood left rich soil. Farmers grew crops in the soil. With more food, more people could live in one place. Cities grew.",
"The first civilizations began beside rivers. Ancient Egypt grew along the Nile River in Africa, and Mesopotamia grew between the Tigris and Euphrates Rivers in Asia. Both depended on a yearly flood that left rich soil for crops. Both built cities, created writing, and had kings. However, they were different in one important way. The Nile flood came at the same time each year, but floods in Mesopotamia were hard to predict and could destroy a village.",
"It is no accident that the earliest civilizations appeared in river valleys. A river provided water for crops, fish, and a route for trade. In Egypt, the Nile flood arrived on a regular schedule and left a layer of rich soil, so farmers could plan their year around it. In Mesopotamia, the land between the Tigris and the Euphrates, floods were less predictable, and people built canals and walls to control the water. Both regions produced more food than farmers needed, which allowed some people to become builders, priests, and traders. Both civilizations also invented writing, partly to keep records of crops and taxes. Their similar solutions suggest that geography shapes the way a society develops.",
[["civilization","a large group of people with cities, laws, and writing"],["river","a long body of moving water"],["flood","water that covers land that is usually dry"],["crops","plants that farmers grow for food"]],
["Which river did Egypt grow beside?","How were the floods different?","Compare the two places. Use both and however."]],
["SS","Needs, Wants, and a Budget","compare",
"A need is something you must have to live. Food is a need. A home is a need. A want is something you would like. A new game is a want. Money is limited. A budget is a plan for your money. First, pay for needs. Then save some money. Last, choose your wants.",
"Everyone has to make choices about money because money is limited. A need is something a person must have to live, such as food, housing, and medicine. A want is something that is nice to have, such as a new phone or concert tickets. A budget is a plan that shows how much money comes in and where it goes. A good budget pays for each need first. Then it sets aside money to save. What is left can be spent on a want.",
"Economics begins with one fact: people want more than they can afford, so they must choose. A need is necessary for survival, such as food, shelter, and health care. A want improves life but is not required. The line between them is not always clear. A phone may be a want for one person and a need for someone who uses it for work. A budget turns these choices into a plan by listing income and expenses. Many advisers suggest paying for needs first, then choosing to save a fixed amount, and only then spending on wants. Every choice has a cost, because money spent on one thing cannot be spent on another. Economists call the thing you give up the opportunity cost.",
[["need","something you must have to live"],["want","something you would like but can live without"],["budget","a plan for how to use your money"],["save","to keep money to use later"]],
["What is a budget?","Is a phone a need or a want? Why?","Compare a need and a want from your own life."]],

["MATH","Percent at the Store","solve",
"Percent means out of one hundred. Fifty percent is half. A store has a sale. A shirt has a price of 20 dollars. The discount is 50 percent. Half of 20 is 10. You save 10 dollars. You pay 10 dollars. Percents help you shop.",
"The word percent means out of one hundred. So 25 percent means 25 out of every hundred, or one fourth. Stores use percents to show a discount, the amount taken off a price. Suppose shoes have a price of 40 dollars and the discount is 25 percent. One fourth of 40 is 10, so you save 10 dollars and pay 30. You can also find 10 percent of any number quickly by moving the decimal point one place to the left.",
"Percents appear on sale signs, test scores, phone batteries, and tax forms, and they all use the same idea: a percent is a ratio out of one hundred. To find a percent of a number, change the percent to a decimal and multiply. A 30 percent discount on a jacket with a price of 60 dollars is 0.30 times 60, or 18 dollars, so the sale price is 42 dollars. A useful shortcut is to find 10 percent first by moving the decimal point one place to the left, and then build other percents from it. Ten percent of 60 is 6, so 30 percent is three times that amount. Estimating with percents helps a shopper check whether a deal is as good as it sounds.",
[["percent","out of one hundred"],["hundred","the number 100"],["discount","money taken off the price"],["price","the money you pay for something"]],
["What does percent mean?","A hat costs 20 dollars. The discount is 10 percent. How much do you save?","Explain your steps to a partner. Use first and then."]],
["MATH","Mean and Median","compare",
"Data is a set of numbers. Here is some data: 2, 3, 10. The mean is the average. Add the numbers. You get 15. Divide by 3. The mean is 5. The median is the middle number. Put the numbers in order. The middle is 3. The median is 3.",
"The mean and the median are two ways to describe a set of data with one number. To find the mean, add all the numbers and divide by how many there are. To find the median, put the numbers in order and choose the middle one. For the data 2, 3, and 10, the mean is 5 and the median is 3. They are different because one large number pulls the mean up, but it does not change the middle.",
"A single number can summarize a whole set of data, but the number you choose matters. The mean is the sum of the values divided by the number of values. The median is the middle value when the data are in order. In many sets they are close. However, an extreme value changes the mean much more than the median. Suppose five workers earn 30, 32, 35, 38, and 200 thousand dollars. The mean is 67 thousand, which is more than four of the five workers earn, while the median is 35 thousand. For this reason, reports about home prices and incomes usually use the median. A careful reader asks which measure was used and why.",
[["data","a set of numbers or facts"],["mean","the average: add the numbers, then divide"],["median","the middle number when the numbers are in order"],["middle","the place halfway between the first and the last"]],
["How do you find the mean?","Find the median of 4, 9, and 5.","When is the median better than the mean? Explain."]],
["MATH","How to Read a Graph","describe",
"A graph is a picture of numbers. A bar graph uses bars. A tall bar means a big number. A short bar means a small number. Each axis is a line on the side or the bottom. One axis has names. The other axis has numbers. The numbers are the scale. Read the title first.",
"A graph turns numbers into a picture so that you can compare them quickly. In a bar graph, each bar stands for one group, and a taller bar means a larger amount. To read it, start with the title. Next, look at each axis, the lines along the bottom and the side, to see what is being measured. Then check the scale, the numbers that show how much each line is worth. Finally, compare the bars and ask what the graph shows.",
"Graphs are powerful because the eye compares shapes faster than the mind compares numbers. That speed can also mislead. A bar graph should be read in order: the title, the labels on each axis, the scale, and only then the bars. The scale deserves special attention. If the numbers on the side begin at 90 instead of 0, a small difference, such as 91 compared with 93, can look enormous because one bar appears three times as tall as the other. The data are true, but the picture exaggerates. Before trusting a graph in an advertisement or a news story, check where the scale starts, what units are used, and who made the graph.",
[["graph","a picture that shows numbers"],["bar","a rectangle on a graph that shows an amount"],["axis","a line on the side or the bottom of a graph"],["scale","the numbers along the side that show the amounts"]],
["What does a tall bar mean?","What should you read first on a graph?","Describe a graph you have seen. What did it show?"]],
["MATH","Ratios in the Kitchen","solve",
"A ratio compares two amounts. A rice recipe uses 1 cup of rice and 2 cups of water. The ratio is 1 to 2. You want more rice. Double the recipe. Use 2 cups of rice. Use 4 cups of water. The ratio stays the same. The rice will taste the same.",
"A ratio compares two amounts. Many cooks make rice with 1 cup of rice for every 2 cups of water, a ratio of 1 to 2. If you need more food, you must keep the ratio the same. To double the recipe, multiply both numbers by 2 and use 2 cups of rice with 4 cups of water. If you change only one number, the ratio changes, and the rice will be too dry or too wet.",
"Cooks, painters, and builders all depend on ratios, comparisons of two amounts that must stay in balance. A common recipe calls for 1 cup of rice to 2 cups of water, a ratio of 1 to 2. Ratios that describe the same relationship are called equivalent ratios: 2 to 4, 3 to 6, and 5 to 10 will all cook the same way. To scale a recipe, multiply or divide both parts by the same number. You can double it for a party or cut it in half for one person. The same thinking solves other problems. If 3 cups of rice feed 8 people, then 24 people, three times as many, need three times as much rice, or 9 cups.",
[["ratio","a way to compare two amounts"],["cup","a tool cooks use to measure"],["double","to make two times as much"],["same","not different"]],
["What is the ratio of rice to water?","How much water do you need for 3 cups of rice?","Tell a partner how to double a recipe from your home."]],
["MATH","Numbers Below Zero","explain",
"Some numbers are less than zero. They are negative numbers. We write them with a small line, like -5. A thermometer shows temperature. Water freezes at zero degrees Celsius. On a very cold day the temperature can go below zero. Five degrees below zero is -5. It is colder than zero.",
"Negative numbers are numbers less than zero. On a number line they are to the left of zero, and we write them with a minus sign. Temperature is a common example. On the Celsius scale, water freezes at zero degrees, so a temperature below freezing is negative. If it is 3 degrees and the temperature drops 5 degrees, it is now 2 degrees below zero, or -2. Negative numbers also describe money that is owed and places below sea level.",
"For centuries many mathematicians refused to accept numbers less than zero, because no one can hold a negative number of apples. Yet negative numbers describe real situations very well. A temperature of -10 degrees Celsius is ten degrees below the point where water freezes. A bank balance of -25 dollars means the account owner owes money. Death Valley in California lies below sea level, so its elevation is written as a negative number. On a number line, values grow smaller to the left, which means -10 is less than -2 even though 10 is greater than 2. Adding a negative number moves left on the line, so 3 plus -5 equals -2, the same as a temperature that falls five degrees from three.",
[["zero","the number 0"],["negative","less than zero"],["below","lower than"],["temperature","how hot or cold something is"]],
["Where are negative numbers on a number line?","It is 2 degrees. The temperature drops 6 degrees. What is it now?","Explain why -10 is less than -2."]],
["MATH","Around and Inside","compare",
"Perimeter is the distance around a shape. Add the length of each side. A room is 4 meters long and 3 meters wide. The perimeter is 4 plus 3 plus 4 plus 3. That is 14 meters. Area is the space inside. Multiply 4 times 3. The area is 12 square meters.",
"Perimeter and area both measure a shape, but they measure different things. Perimeter is the distance around the outside, so you add the length of every side. Area is the amount of space inside, so for a rectangle you multiply the length by the width. A garden that is 5 meters long and 2 meters wide has a perimeter of 14 meters and an area of 10 square meters. You need the perimeter to buy a fence, but you need the area to buy soil.",
"Perimeter and area are easy to confuse because both come from the same measurements, but they answer different questions. Perimeter is a length: the total distance around a figure, measured in units such as meters. Area is a surface: the number of square units that cover the figure. Two rectangles can have the same perimeter and different areas. A rectangle 6 meters by 2 meters and a square 4 meters on each side both have a perimeter of 16 meters, yet the first has an area of 12 square meters and the second has 16. A farmer with a fixed length of fence can therefore enclose more land with a square than with a long, thin rectangle.",
[["perimeter","the distance around a shape"],["area","the space inside a flat shape"],["length","how long something is"],["square","a shape with four equal sides; also the unit for area"]],
["What does perimeter measure?","A rectangle is 3 meters by 2 meters. What is its area?","When do you need area and not perimeter? Give an example."]],
["MATH","What Are the Chances?","explain",
"Probability tells how likely something is. Toss a coin. It can land on heads or tails. Each side has an equal chance. The chance of heads is 1 out of 2. Roll a number cube. It has 6 sides. The chance of a 4 is 1 out of 6. A 4 is less likely than heads.",
"Probability is a number that tells how likely an event is. It can be written as a fraction from 0, which means impossible, to 1, which means certain. A coin has two sides with an equal chance, so the probability of heads is 1 out of 2. A number cube has six sides, so the probability of rolling a 4 is 1 out of 6. Probability does not tell you what will happen next. It tells you what to expect after many tries.",
"Weather forecasts, insurance prices, and medical tests all rely on probability, the measure of how likely an event is. When every outcome has an equal chance, the probability of an event is the number of ways it can happen divided by the number of possible outcomes. A coin gives heads with a probability of one half. That does not mean heads and tails take turns. Each toss is independent, so after five heads in a row, the chance of heads on the next toss is still one half. Many people believe a different result is due, a mistake known as the gambler's fallacy. Probability describes what is likely over a large number of trials, not what must happen in the next one.",
[["probability","a number that tells how likely something is"],["chance","the possibility that something will happen"],["likely","expected to happen"],["equal","the same in amount"]],
["What is the chance of heads?","Which is more likely: heads on a coin or a 4 on a number cube?","Explain why five heads in a row do not change the next toss."]],
["MATH","The Story of Zero","summarize",
"Zero is a number. It means none. Long ago, some people had no sign for zero. Counting was hard. People in India used zero as a number long ago. Zero also holds a place. Look at 205. The digit 0 shows there are no tens. Without zero, 205 looks like 25.",
"Today zero seems simple, but for a long time many number systems did not have it. People could count things, but they had no number for nothing. Mathematicians in India began to treat zero as a real number, and about 1,400 years ago one of them wrote rules for adding and subtracting with it. Zero also has a second job. It holds a place in a number. In 205, the digit 0 shows that there are no tens, so we can tell 205 from 25.",
"Zero is one of the most important inventions in mathematics, and it was invented more than once. The ancient Babylonians and the Maya each used a symbol to hold an empty place in a number. In India, mathematicians went further and treated zero as a number of its own. In the year 628, the scholar Brahmagupta wrote rules for calculating with it. The idea traveled through the Arabic-speaking world and reached Europe centuries later, where it replaced Roman numerals for most work. Zero matters because of place value: each digit in a number is worth ten times the digit to its right, and zero keeps the other digits in the correct place. Without it, modern arithmetic and computers would be impossible.",
[["zero","the number 0; none"],["number","a word or sign that tells how many"],["digit","one of the signs 0 to 9"],["place","the position of a digit in a number"]],
["What does zero mean?","What job does zero do in 205?","Summarize the story of zero in two sentences."]],
["MATH","Close Enough: Estimating","explain",
"An estimate is a smart guess. It is not exact. It is about right. To estimate, round the numbers. You buy milk for 3 dollars and 89 cents. Round it to 4 dollars. You buy bread for 2 dollars and 10 cents. Round it to 2 dollars. You need about 6 dollars.",
"An estimate is a careful guess that is close to the exact answer. People estimate when they do not need an exact number or want to check their work. One way is to round each number to one that is easy to use. If three items cost 3.89, 2.10, and 4.95 dollars, you can round them to 4, 2, and 5 and say the total is about 11 dollars. If a calculator gives 110, the estimate tells you that something went wrong.",
"In school, students usually look for the exact answer, but outside school an estimate is often more useful. A shopper wants to know whether twenty dollars is enough, and a driver wants to know about how long a trip will take. Estimating starts when you round numbers to values that are easy to work with. A trip of 289 miles at 62 miles an hour is close to 300 divided by 60, or about 5 hours. An estimate is also a safety check. If a calculator shows that the same trip takes 50 hours, the estimate reveals that a key was pressed by mistake. Scientists and engineers estimate first for the same reason: a quick, sensible number protects them from trusting a wrong one.",
[["estimate","a careful guess that is close to the answer"],["exact","completely correct, not just close"],["round","to change a number to a close number that is easy to use"],["about","close to, but not exactly"]],
["What is an estimate?","Estimate 4.95 plus 3.10.","When is an estimate better than an exact answer? Explain."]],
["MATH","Patterns That Grow","sequence",
"A pattern follows a rule. Ana saves 5 dollars each week. After week 1 she has 5 dollars. After week 2 she has 10 dollars. After week 3 she has 15 dollars. The rule is add 5 each week. A table shows the pattern. After week 10 she has 50 dollars.",
"A pattern is a set of numbers that follows a rule. Suppose Ana saves 5 dollars each week. First she has 5 dollars, next 10, then 15. You can show the pattern in a table with one column for weeks and one for dollars. The rule is to multiply the number of weeks by 5. With the rule, you do not have to count every week. After 20 weeks, Ana will have 20 times 5, or 100 dollars.",
"Mathematics is often called the study of patterns, and one of the most useful is the pattern of steady change. If Ana saves 5 dollars each week, her savings form the sequence 5, 10, 15, 20. A table makes the pattern visible, and a rule makes it powerful: savings equal 5 times the number of weeks. Written with symbols, the rule is y = 5x. If she begins with 20 dollars already in the bank, the rule becomes y = 5x + 20. On a graph, the points fall on a straight line, so this kind of pattern is called linear. The rule answers questions a table cannot answer quickly, such as how much she will have after a year or when she will reach 300 dollars.",
[["pattern","numbers or shapes that follow a rule"],["each","every one"],["rule","what tells you how to get the next number"],["table","rows and columns that organize numbers"]],
["How much does Ana save each week?","How much will she have after 6 weeks?","Make your own saving pattern. Tell the rule."]],

["ELA","Can You Trust This Source?","argue",
"A source is where you get information. Some sources are reliable. You can trust them. Ask three questions. Who is the author? Is the author an expert? Is there evidence, like facts and numbers? Is the date new? If you cannot find an author, be careful. Check a second source.",
"The internet has millions of pages, but not all of them are true. A reliable source is one you can trust. To check a source, ask a few questions. Who is the author, and is that person an expert? Does the page give evidence, such as facts, data, or the names of studies? When was it written? Why was it written: to inform or to sell something? If you are not sure, look for the same information in a second source.",
"Anyone can publish a website, so readers have to do the work that editors once did. A reliable source usually shows several signs. It names an author with real knowledge of the subject. It supports its claims with evidence that a reader can check, such as data, documents, or quotations from experts. It gives a date, and it corrects its mistakes. Purpose matters as well. A page that exists to sell a product or to make readers angry may leave out facts that do not fit. Professional fact-checkers use a habit called lateral reading: instead of studying one page closely, they open new tabs and see what other sources say about it. No single test is perfect, but several quick checks together catch most false claims.",
[["source","where information comes from"],["reliable","able to be trusted"],["author","the person who wrote something"],["evidence","facts that show something is true"]],
["What is a source?","Name two questions to ask about a source.","Is a video from a stranger a reliable source? Give a reason."]],
["ELA","Fact or Opinion?","compare",
"A fact is true. You can prove it. Jackson is the capital of Mississippi. That is a fact. An opinion is what a person thinks or feels. You cannot prove it. Jackson is the best city. That is an opinion. Words like best, worst, and I believe show an opinion.",
"Good readers can tell a fact from an opinion. A fact is a statement that you can prove with evidence. For example, Jackson is the capital of Mississippi. An opinion tells what someone thinks, feels, or believes, and other people may disagree. For example, Jackson is the best city in the South. Signal words such as best, worst, should, and I believe often show an opinion. Both are useful, but a strong opinion is supported by facts.",
"The difference between a fact and an opinion sounds simple, yet it is the base of careful reading. A fact is a statement that can be checked: you can prove it true or false with evidence. An opinion is a judgment that depends on what a person values, so reasonable people can disagree about it. Writers often mix the two in one sentence. In the sentence 'The school added a 20-minute reading period, which wastes valuable time,' the first part can be checked and the second part is a judgment. Opinions are not worthless. Editorials, reviews, and arguments are built on them. The question for a reader is whether writers support what they believe with facts or only repeat it more loudly.",
[["fact","something that is true and can be checked"],["opinion","what a person thinks or feels"],["prove","to show that something is true"],["believe","to think that something is true"]],
["What is a fact?","Which words show an opinion?","Say one fact and one opinion about your school."]],
["ELA","Words English Borrowed","explain",
"English has many words from other places. A language can borrow a word. English took rodeo and patio from Spanish. It took pizza from Italian. It took kindergarten from German. You may know some English words already. Animal and hospital look the same in Spanish and English. Your first language helps you.",
"English is full of words that came from other languages. When people trade, travel, and move, each language will borrow from the others. English took rodeo, patio, and tornado from Spanish, pizza from Italian, and kindergarten from German. Many other English words share a root with Spanish because both languages took them from Latin. Animal, hospital, and family are easy for a Spanish speaker to recognize. A word like these is called a cognate, and noticing cognates is a fast way to grow your vocabulary.",
"English has one of the largest vocabularies of any language, largely because it has never been shy about borrowing. After French-speaking Normans conquered England in 1066, thousands of French words entered English. Later, trade and migration added more: rodeo and patio from Spanish, piano from Italian, kindergarten from German, and pajamas from South Asia. Scholars also built new terms from Latin and Greek roots. For multilingual students this history is an advantage. Spanish and English share thousands of cognates, words with a common origin and a similar form, such as nation and nación. A reader who notices that a new English word resembles a word in another language can often borrow its meaning, although a few false friends, like embarazada and embarrassed, do not match.",
[["language","the words people use to speak and write"],["borrow","to take and use something from someone else"],["word","a group of letters or sounds with a meaning"],["Spanish","the language of Spain and much of Latin America"]],
["Name one English word that came from Spanish.","What is a cognate?","Share a word from your home language that looks like an English word."]],
["ELA","Two Languages, One Brain","explain",
"A bilingual person uses two languages. Your brain keeps both languages ready. You choose one and hold back the other. You can switch from one to the other. This is good practice for the brain. It is like exercise. Being bilingual also helps you talk with more people. It is a strength.",
"A bilingual person can use two languages. Scientists have learned that both languages stay active in the brain at the same time. When a bilingual person speaks, the brain must choose the right language and hold back the other one. People who switch languages do this many times a day. Some researchers think this constant practice strengthens attention. Being bilingual has clear benefits too. It lets people speak with more family members, read more, and qualify for more jobs.",
"For many years, some schools told families that two languages would confuse a child. Research has not supported that fear. Studies show that both languages remain active in the bilingual brain, even when only one is in use. To speak, a person must select one language and hold back the other, and must switch when the listener changes. Some researchers argue that this daily practice improves attention and the ability to move between tasks, although scientists still debate how large the effect is. Other benefits are not in doubt. Bilingual adults can communicate across communities, learn additional languages more easily, and qualify for jobs that require two languages. Many states now award a Seal of Biliteracy to graduates who show skill in two languages.",
[["bilingual","able to use two languages"],["brain","the part of your body that thinks and learns"],["switch","to change from one thing to another"],["practice","doing something many times to get better"]],
["What does bilingual mean?","What must the brain do when a bilingual person speaks?","How does knowing two languages help you? Give two ways."]],
["ELA","The Parts of a News Article","describe",
"A news article has parts. The headline is the title. It is big. It tells the main idea. The lead is the first sentence. It tells who, what, when, and where. A reporter writes the article. The reporter talks to people. A quote is the exact words a person said. A quote has quotation marks around it.",
"A news article is organized so that readers get the most important facts first. The headline is the title, and it gives the main idea in a few words. The lead is the first sentence or two, and it answers the questions who, what, when, and where. The paragraphs that follow add details and explain why and how. A reporter gathers these facts by interviewing people. When the article repeats a person's exact words, that is a quote, and it appears inside quotation marks.",
"News writing follows a shape that journalists call the inverted pyramid: the most important information comes first and the least important comes last. The headline is written to catch attention and state the main point. The lead, usually a single sentence, answers as many of the basic questions as possible: who, what, when, where, why, and how. Later paragraphs add background and details. This order lets a busy reader stop at any point and still know the main facts. A reporter is also expected to show where information came from. A quote gives a person's exact words inside quotation marks, and a phrase such as 'according to the mayor' tells readers who is responsible for a claim. Knowing these parts helps a reader separate what happened from what someone says about it.",
[["headline","the title of a news article"],["lead","the first sentence, which gives the main facts"],["reporter","a person who finds and writes the news"],["quote","the exact words a person said"]],
["What is a headline?","What questions does the lead answer?","Find a headline today. What do you think the article is about?"]],
["ELA","How to Email a Teacher","sequence",
"You can send an email to a teacher. First, write a subject. It tells what the email is about. Next, write a greeting, like Dear Mr. Lee. Then write your message. Keep it short. Say what you need. Be polite. Say please and thank you. Last, write your name.",
"A clear email helps you get a quick answer. First, write a subject line that tells what the email is about, such as 'Question about Friday's homework.' Next, begin with a greeting and the teacher's name. Then write your message in two or three sentences. Say who you are, what you need, and when you need it. Use polite words such as please and thank you. Finally, end with a closing and your full name, and read the email once before you send it.",
"Writing to a teacher, a coach, or an employer is different from texting a friend, and the difference can affect the answer you receive. A formal email has a clear order. The subject line states the purpose in a few words so that the reader can find the email later. The greeting uses the person's title and last name. The message gets to the point in the first sentence, gives only the details the reader needs, and makes a specific request. A polite tone matters: complete sentences, no slang, and a word of thanks. The closing gives your full name and, for a teacher, your class period. Finally, proofread. One short, careful email usually gets a faster and friendlier reply than three messages sent in a hurry.",
[["subject","the short line that tells what an email is about"],["greeting","the words that begin a letter or email, such as Dear"],["message","what you want to tell someone"],["polite","showing good manners and respect"]],
["What goes in the subject line?","Why should the message be short?","Tell the steps to write an email. Use first, next, then, and last."]],
["ELA","Text Features Help You Read","explain",
"Informational books have text features. They help you read. A heading is a title for one part. It tells the topic. Bold words are dark. They are important words. A caption is the words under a picture. It tells about the picture. A diagram is a picture with labels. Look at these before you read.",
"Informational texts use text features to help readers find and understand ideas. A heading is the title of a section, and it tells what the section is about. Words in bold print are key terms you need to know. A caption explains a photograph or drawing. A diagram is a labeled picture that shows the parts of something or how it works. Before you read, look at all of these. They give you the main ideas and help you guess what the text will say.",
"A textbook page is designed to be used, not only read from top to bottom. Its text features are tools. A heading divides the chapter into parts and works like a map of the ideas. Bold print marks the vocabulary that the author expects you to learn. A caption connects a photograph to the topic, and a diagram can show a process, such as the water cycle, more clearly than a paragraph can. Strong readers preview these features before reading. In about two minutes they learn the topic, the main ideas, and the key terms, and they turn each heading into a question to answer. Reading with a question in mind makes the text easier to understand and easier to remember.",
[["heading","the title of one part of a text"],["bold","darker, thicker print for important words"],["caption","the words that explain a picture"],["diagram","a picture with labels that shows parts or steps"]],
["What is a caption?","Why are some words in bold print?","Open a textbook. Find one heading and one diagram. Tell what they show."]],
["ELA","From Pictures to the Alphabet","sequence",
"Long ago, people had no writing. They told stories out loud. Then people drew pictures. Later, people used symbols. Each symbol stood for a word or an idea. There were hundreds of symbols. After that, people made an alphabet. Each letter stands for one of the sounds we say. With an alphabet, people need only a few letters.",
"Writing was invented about 5,000 years ago. First, people in Mesopotamia pressed marks into wet clay to keep records of grain and animals. In Egypt, people carved picture symbols called hieroglyphs. These early systems had hundreds of symbols, so only a few trained people could read. Later, about 3,000 years ago, traders called the Phoenicians used an alphabet of about 22 letters, each standing for one of the sounds of their language. Because an alphabet is small, many more people could learn it.",
"For most of human history, knowledge lasted only as long as someone remembered it. Writing changed that. About 5,000 years ago, people in Mesopotamia began pressing wedge-shaped marks into clay to record trade, and Egyptians developed hieroglyphs soon after. These systems used hundreds of symbols for words and syllables, so reading remained the skill of a small group of trained scribes. The next great step was the alphabet. Around 3,000 years ago the Phoenicians, a trading people, spread a set of about 22 letters that stood for the sounds of speech. The Greeks added vowels, and the Romans adapted the Greek letters into the alphabet used for English and Spanish today. A system with so few symbols could be learned by ordinary people, and literacy slowly spread.",
[["writing","words or marks put on a surface to be read"],["symbols","marks or pictures that stand for something"],["alphabet","the set of letters used to write a language"],["sounds","what you hear when someone speaks"]],
["Where did people first press marks into clay?","Why was an alphabet easier to learn?","Retell how writing changed. Use first, later, and after that."]],
["ELA","How to Write a Summary","summarize",
"A summary is short. It tells the most important part of a text. First, read the text. Next, find the main idea. Ask: what is this mostly about? Then choose two or three important details. Leave out small details. Last, write the summary in your own words. Do not copy.",
"A summary is a short retelling of the most important information in a text. To write one, first read the whole text. Next, find the main idea by asking what the text is mostly about. Then choose the two or three details that best support it, and leave out examples and small facts. Finally, write the summary in your own words. A good summary is much shorter than the text, and it does not include your opinion.",
"Summarizing is one of the best ways to check whether you understood what you read, because you cannot shorten a text that you did not follow. A summary states the main idea and the most important details and leaves out everything else. Begin by reading the full text and asking what point the author most wants readers to remember. Then select only the details that the main idea depends on. Examples, repeated points, and interesting side facts can go. Write the summary in your own words: closing the text while you write helps you avoid copying. Finally, keep yourself out of it. A summary reports what the author said, not whether you agree. A useful test is length. If the summary is more than about one fourth as long as the original, it probably includes too much.",
[["summary","a short retelling of the most important parts"],["main idea","what a text is mostly about"],["details","small pieces of information"],["own words","the way you would say it, not copied"]],
["What is a summary?","What should you leave out of a summary?","Summarize a text you read this week in two sentences."]],
["ELA","Read More, Know More Words","cause",
"Do you want to know more words? Read every day. Books have more new words than talk does. When you read, you see the same words many times. Then you remember them. Read for 20 minutes each day. Read what you like. Read in any language. Your vocabulary will grow.",
"One of the best ways to build your vocabulary is to read every day. Books and articles use many words that people rarely say in conversation. When you read, you meet these words again and again in different sentences, so you slowly learn what they mean. Even 20 minutes a day adds up to more than 120 hours in a year. As a result, students who read often know more words, and knowing more words makes the next book easier to read.",
"Teachers can explain only a few hundred words in a school year, yet students learn thousands. Most of those words come from reading. Written language uses a wider range of words than everyday speech, so people who read meet words that conversation rarely offers. Each meeting adds a little to the reader's understanding, and after several meetings in different contexts the word becomes familiar. The effect builds on itself. Because strong readers know more vocabulary, they read more easily and choose to read more, which teaches them still more words. The habit does not require hours. Twenty minutes a day comes to more than 120 hours a year. Reading in a home language counts as well, since knowledge and many reading skills transfer from one language to another.",
[["read","to look at words and understand them"],["words","groups of letters that have a meaning"],["vocabulary","all the words a person knows"],["minutes","short units of time; 60 make one hour"]],
["How many minutes should you read each day?","Why do books teach more words than talk does?","What do you like to read? Tell why."]]
];
const TXV=[["em","Levels 1-2",3],["pr","Levels 3-4",4],["l5","Level 5",5]];
const TXS=[["","My plan's subject"],["ALL","Any subject"],["ELA","English Language Arts"],["MATH","Math"],["SCI","Science"],["SS","Social Studies"]];
const TXN={SCI:"Science",SS:"Social Studies",MATH:"Math",ELA:"English Language Arts"};
const TXD=["Sunday","Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday","Saturday"];
/* interleave the four subjects so "Any subject" changes subject every day */
const TX=[];(function(){const by={};TXRAW.forEach(r=>(by[r[0]]=by[r[0]]||[]).push(r));for(let i=0;i<10;i++)["SCI","SS","MATH","ELA"].forEach(s=>{if(by[s][i])TX.push(by[s][i]);});})();
let TOFF=0,TSAY="";
/* bilingual support: on by default when Spanish is one of the home languages in the class */
function bi(){return S.bi===true||(S.bi==null&&S.langs.includes("Spanish"));}
function biChips(){const b=bi();return `<div class="chips noprint noshow bic"><button class="chip ${b?"":"on"}" data-bi="0" aria-pressed="${!b}">English only</button><button class="chip ${b?"on":""}" data-bi="1" aria-pressed="${b}">English + Spanish</button></div>`;}
function esp(s){return `<span class="es" lang="es">${esc(s)}</span>`;}
function txEsKey(lv){return lv==="em"?"em":"pr";}
function txEsBody(r,lv){const s=TXES[r[1]];return lv==="em"?s[1]:s[2];}
function txEsLab(lv){return lv==="em"?"Niveles 1-2":lv==="pr"?"Niveles 3-4":"Niveles 3-4 · versión más corta que el texto en inglés";}
function txState(){if(!S.tx||typeof S.tx!=="object")S.tx={on:true,s:"",lv:"",pin:-1};const t=S.tx;if(typeof t.pin!=="number"||t.pin>=TX.length)t.pin=-1;return t;}
/* school days (Monday to Friday) from Monday, Aug 3, 2026; Saturday and Sunday count as the Friday before */
function schoolDays(d){const n=Math.floor((Date.UTC(d.getFullYear(),d.getMonth(),d.getDate())-Date.UTC(2026,7,3))/864e5);return Math.floor(n/7)*5+Math.min(((n%7)+7)%7,4);}
function txDay(off){const d=new Date();d.setHours(12,0,0,0);while(d.getDay()===0||d.getDay()===6)d.setDate(d.getDate()-1);let k=Math.abs(off);const st=off<0?-1:1;while(k>0){d.setDate(d.getDate()+st);if(d.getDay()!==0&&d.getDay()!==6)k--;}return d;}
function txSubj(){const t=txState();return t.s||S.s;}
function txList(){const s=txSubj();return TX.map((r,i)=>i).filter(i=>s==="ALL"||TX[i][0]===s);}
function txIndex(){const t=txState();if(t.pin>=0)return t.pin;const L=txList(),n=schoolDays(new Date())+TOFF;return L[((n%L.length)+L.length)%L.length];}
function txLevel(){const t=txState();if(t.lv)return t.lv;const g=groups();return g.em>=g.pr&&g.em>=g.l5&&g.em?"em":g.l5>g.pr?"l5":"pr";}
function txBody(r,lv){return r[lv==="em"?3:lv==="pr"?4:5];}
function txWords(s){return s.trim().split(/\s+/).length;}
function txFrames(fnk,lv){const fr=F[fnk].fr;return lv==="em"?[[1,fr[0]],[2,fr[1]]]:lv==="pr"?[[3,fr[2]],[4,fr[3]]]:[[5,fr[4]]];}
function txRoom(){const g=groups(),o=TXV.filter(v=>g[v[0]]>0).map(v=>v[0]);return o.length?o:[txLevel()];}
function txWhen(plain){const t=txState();if(t.pin>=0)return "Kept for this plan";const d=txDay(TOFF),lab=TXD[d.getDay()]+", "+nice(d.getFullYear()+"-"+String(d.getMonth()+1).padStart(2,"0")+"-"+String(d.getDate()).padStart(2,"0"));return (TOFF===0&&!plain?"Today's text · ":"")+lab;}
function txPlan(){const t=txState();if(!t.on)return[];const r=TX[txIndex()],b=bi(),s=TXES[r[1]],L=["",`Daily informational text (${txWhen(true)}): ${r[1]}${b?" / "+s[0]:""} [${TXN[r[0]]}; ${F[r[2]].label.toLowerCase()}]`],done=[];
 txRoom().forEach(lv=>{const v=TXV.find(x=>x[0]===lv);L.push(` ${v[1]} version (${txWords(txBody(r,lv))} words): ${txBody(r,lv)}`);if(b&&!done.includes(txEsKey(lv))){done.push(txEsKey(lv));L.push(` En español (${txEsLab(lv)}): ${txEsBody(r,lv)}`);}});
 L.push(" Key words: "+r[6].map((w,j)=>w[0]+(b?" / "+s[3][j][0]:"")+" ("+w[1]+(b?" / "+s[3][j][1]:"")+")").join("; "));r[7].forEach((q,i)=>L.push(` Question ${i+1}: ${q}${b?" / "+s[4][i]:""}`));return L;}
function txCard(){const t=txState(),i=txIndex(),r=TX[i],lv=txLevel(),g=groups(),L=txList(),pos=L.indexOf(i)+1,sub=txSubj(),body=txBody(r,lv),say=("speechSynthesis" in window),b=bi(),s=TXES[r[1]],fe=FES[r[2]],
 others=txRoom().filter(x=>x!==lv),done=[txEsKey(lv)],tags=[["Find it","Busca"],["Think","Piensa"],["Talk or write","Habla o escribe"]],
 esArt=l=>`<article class="tx esx" lang="es"><p class="txk">En español · ${txEsLab(l)} · ${txWords(txEsBody(r,l))} palabras</p><h4>${esc(s[0])}</h4><p>${esc(txEsBody(r,l))}</p></article>`;
 return `<div class="card ${t.on?"keep":""}" id="txcard"><h3>Daily text developer</h3><p class="hint noshow">A new informational text every school day, written at three English proficiency levels with key words, questions, and sentence frames. Tap to change the subject, the level, the languages, or the day.</p>
 <div class="noshow noprint"><span class="lbl">Subject</span><div class="chips">${TXS.map(x=>`<button class="chip ${t.s===x[0]?"on":""}" data-txs="${x[0]||"PLAN"}" aria-pressed="${t.s===x[0]}">${x[0]?x[1]:x[1]+": "+TXN[S.s]}</button>`).join("")}</div>
 <span class="lbl">Reading level</span><div class="chips">${TXV.map(v=>`<button class="chip ${lv===v[0]?"on":""}" data-txl="${v[0]}" aria-pressed="${lv===v[0]}">${v[1]}${g[v[0]]?" · "+g[v[0]]+" in class":""}</button>`).join("")}</div>
 <span class="lbl">Languages</span>${biChips()}
 <div class="txnav"><button class="btn ghost" data-txd="-1" ${t.pin>=0?"disabled":""} aria-label="Earlier school day">‹ Earlier</button><b>${esc(txWhen())}</b><button class="btn ghost" data-txd="1" ${t.pin>=0?"disabled":""} aria-label="Later school day">Later ›</button>${TOFF!==0&&t.pin<0?'<button class="btn ghost" data-txd="0">Today</button>':""}</div></div>
 <article class="tx" id="txtext"><p class="txk">${esc(TXN[r[0]])} · ${F[r[2]].label} · ${TXV.find(v=>v[0]===lv)[1]} · ${txWords(body)} words</p><h4>${esc(r[1])}</h4><p>${esc(body)}</p></article>
 ${b?esArt(lv):""}
 <h4 class="acat">Key words${b?" · Palabras clave":""}</h4><dl class="txw">${r[6].map((w,j)=>`<div><dt>${esc(w[0])}${b?` · <span lang="es">${esc(s[3][j][0])}</span>`:""}</dt><dd>${esc(w[1])}${b?esp(s[3][j][1]):""}</dd></div>`).join("")}</dl>
 <h4 class="acat">Questions${b?" · Preguntas":""}</h4><ol class="txq">${r[7].map((q,j)=>`<li><span class="g all">${tags[j][0]}${b?" · "+tags[j][1]:""}</span>${esc(q)}${b?esp(s[4][j]):""}</li>`).join("")}</ol>
 <h4 class="acat">Sentence frames: ${F[r[2]].label}${b?" · Marcos de oraciones: "+fe.label:""}</h4><div class="fr">${txFrames(r[2],lv).map(x=>`<div class="x${x[0]}"><b>Level ${x[0]}</b><span>${esc(x[1])}${b?esp(fe.fr[x[0]-1]):""}</span></div>`).join("")}</div>
 ${others.length?`<div class="onlyprint">${others.map(o=>{let h=`<article class="tx"><p class="txk">${TXV.find(v=>v[0]===o)[1]} · ${txWords(txBody(r,o))} words</p><h4>${esc(r[1])}</h4><p>${esc(txBody(r,o))}</p></article>`;if(b&&!done.includes(txEsKey(o))){done.push(txEsKey(o));h+=esArt(o);}return h;}).join("")}</div>`:""}
 <div class="row noprint noshow">${say?`<button class="btn" id="txsay">${TSAY==="en"?"Stop":"Listen"}</button>`:""}${say&&b?`<button class="btn" id="txsayes">${TSAY==="es"?"Parar":"Escuchar en español"}</button>`:""}<button class="btn ghost" id="txcopy">Copy this text</button><button class="btn ghost" id="txon" aria-pressed="${!!t.on}">${t.on?"In my plan ✓":"Add to my plan"}</button><button class="btn ghost" id="txpin" aria-pressed="${t.pin>=0}">${t.pin>=0?"Kept ✓ Tap to change daily":"Keep this text"}</button></div><textarea id="txbox" class="cbox" rows="8" readonly aria-label="Daily text"></textarea>
 <p class="note noshow">Text ${pos} of ${L.length} in ${sub==="ALL"?"all subjects":esc(TXN[sub])}. The text changes each school day and the set starts over after ${L.length} school days. When the text is in your plan, it is added to the copied plan, the printout, and the board view. Printing includes a version for every level in your class. The levels are English proficiency levels, not grade levels. Spanish is written for the Levels 1-2 and Levels 3-4 versions; Level 5 shows the Levels 3-4 Spanish. Supports ELP standards 1 and 8.</p></div>`;}
function txCopyText(){const r=TX[txIndex()],lv=txLevel(),b=bi(),s=TXES[r[1]],fe=FES[r[2]],L=[r[1],"",txBody(r,lv)];if(b)L.push("",s[0],"",txEsBody(r,lv));L.push("","Key words"+(b?" / Palabras clave":""));r[6].forEach((w,j)=>L.push(" "+w[0]+(b?" / "+s[3][j][0]:"")+": "+w[1]+(b?" / "+s[3][j][1]:"")));L.push("","Questions"+(b?" / Preguntas":""));r[7].forEach((q,i)=>{L.push(" "+(i+1)+". "+q);if(b)L.push("    "+s[4][i]);});L.push("","Sentence frames"+(b?" / Marcos de oraciones":""));txFrames(r[2],lv).forEach(x=>{L.push(" "+x[1]);if(b)L.push("    "+fe.fr[x[0]-1]);});return L.join("\n");}
function txStop(){try{window.speechSynthesis.cancel();}catch(e){}TSAY="";}
function txSpeak(which,b){if(TSAY===which){txStop();keep();return;}try{const r=TX[txIndex()],lv=txLevel(),en=which==="en",u=new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(en?r[1]+". "+txBody(r,lv):TXES[r[1]][0]+". "+txEsBody(r,lv));u.lang=en?"en-US":"es-US";u.rate=lv==="em"?.8:lv==="pr"?.9:1;u.onend=u.onerror=()=>{if(TSAY===which){TSAY="";const x=$(en?"txsay":"txsayes");if(x)x.textContent=en?"Listen":"Escuchar en español";}};window.speechSynthesis.cancel();TSAY=which;keep();window.speechSynthesis.speak(u);}catch(err){TSAY="";}}
document.addEventListener("click",e=>{const b=e.target.closest("button");if(!b)return;const d=b.dataset,t=txState();
 if(d.txs){txStop();t.s=d.txs==="PLAN"?"":d.txs;t.pin=-1;keep();}
 else if(d.txl){txStop();t.lv=d.txl;keep();}
 else if(d.txd){txStop();TOFF=d.txd==="0"?0:TOFF+(+d.txd);keep();}
 else if(d.bi){txStop();S.bi=d.bi==="1";keep();}
 else if(b.id==="txon"){t.on=!t.on;keep();}
 else if(b.id==="txpin"){t.pin=t.pin>=0?-1:txIndex();keep();}
 else if(b.id==="txcopy"){const tx=txCopyText();const fb=ok=>{b.textContent=ok?"Copied":"Select the text below and copy it";if(!ok){const x=$("txbox");x.style.display="block";x.value=tx;x.focus();x.select();}};try{navigator.clipboard.writeText(tx).then(()=>fb(true),()=>fb(false));}catch(err){fb(false);}}
 else if(b.id==="txsay"){txSpeak("en");}
 else if(b.id==="txsayes"){txSpeak("es");}
});
